# Exercise: Measure λ (Throughput)
#
# Little's Law: L = λW
#   λ = the rate at which items leave the system (items per unit time)
#
# In ex 1 we measured W with one worker and one item. Now we measure
# what happens with N parallel workers: throughput should rise.
#
# Setup: 4 workers, each sleeps 10ms (W stays 10ms). We send 4 items
# at once. If the workers truly run in parallel, wall-clock time is
# close to ONE worker's service time, not four times it.
#
#   Serial would be:   ~4 × 10ms = ~40_000us   →  λ ≈ 100 items/sec
#   Parallel should be: ~10ms      = ~10_000us  →  λ ≈ 400 items/sec
#
# We assert wall time < 25_000us (25ms). Crossing that threshold means
# workers really did overlap — proves λ scaled with worker count.
#
# Stack-shuffling notes:
#   `N pick` copies the element N positions down to the top, leaving
#   the original in place — so you can reach a channel many times
#   without consuming it. When the stack is ( work results start ),
#   pushing a value gives ( work results start v ). At that point the
#   value is at depth 0, start at 1, results at 2, and work at 3 — so
#   `3 pick` copies work onto the top, ready for `chan.send` which
#   wants ( value channel ).
#
# Your task: bracket the send-and-receive with `time.now`, run all 4
# items through the workers, compute elapsed time, leave it on top.
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: test-throughput ( -- )
    chan.make   # work
    chan.make   # results
    # Stack: ( work results )

    # Spawn 4 one-shot workers: each receives one item, sleeps 10ms,
    # adds 1, sends result, exits.
    [ over chan.receive drop 10 time.sleep-ms 1 i.+ swap chan.send drop drop ] strand.spawn drop
    [ over chan.receive drop 10 time.sleep-ms 1 i.+ swap chan.send drop drop ] strand.spawn drop
    [ over chan.receive drop 10 time.sleep-ms 1 i.+ swap chan.send drop drop ] strand.spawn drop
    [ over chan.receive drop 10 time.sleep-ms 1 i.+ swap chan.send drop drop ] strand.spawn drop

    # Stack going in: ( work results )
    # Your code here:
    # - take a start timestamp with time.now
    # - send 4 items: 10, 20, 30, 40 — push the value first, then
    #   `3 pick` to copy the work channel above it
    # - receive 4 results, discarding the value each time —
    #   `1 pick` reaches the results channel (nothing pushed above it)
    # - take another timestamp and subtract start to get elapsed
    # - clean the two channels off the stack
    # Stack coming out: ( elapsed )    -- elapsed time in us
    drop drop 0

    # Do not edit below this line
    # elapsed should be < 25_000us (25ms) — proves parallelism.
    # Serial execution would be ~40_000us; well above the threshold.
    25000 i.< test.assert
;
